  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nWe aim to improve the upper bound for C\u2085 by leveraging frequency domain analysis and gradient-based optimization. The key idea is to exploit the problem's structure via Fourier transforms and explore multiple initialization strategies to escape local optima.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nWe represent h as an n_points-dimensional vector. Define:\n- h\u0302 = FFT(h)\n- (1 - h)\u0302 = FFT(1 - h)\nThe overlap integral becomes max(|h\u0302 * (1 - h)\u0302*|) * (2/n_points) / n_points.\n\nThe objective is to minimize this maximum value while satisfying:\n0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 and \u03a3h[i] = n_points / 2.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na. Initialize:\n   - Start with initial_h_values if provided. Else, create a symmetric linear ramp.\n   - Ensure feasibility: Clip and scale h to meet constraints.\n\nb. Fractional factorial search:\n   - Generate 20 candidates using FFT and evaluate their C\u2085 bounds.\n   - Select top 5 with lowest C\u2085 for gradient descent.\n\nc. Gradient descent:\n   - Compute gradients in frequency domain using h\u0302 and (1 - h)\u0302.\n   - Update h using Projected Gradient Descent.\n   - Apply FFT-based step and project onto feasible region.\n\nd. Iterate:\n   - Alternate between fractional factorial sampling and gradient descent.\n   - Track best candidate.\n\ne. Termination:\n   - Return best h found before time expires.\n\n4. Search choices and variations\n- Start with symmetric initializations to exploit problem symmetry.\n- Use controlled noise addition for global exploration.\n- Default n_points = 70 for efficiency.\n\n5. Compute budget\n- Fractional factorial search: ~400s\n- Gradient descent: ~600s\n- Alternating schedule ensures progress within 1100s.\n\n6. Validation\n- Ensure feasibility after each step.\n- Use exact evaluator to validate final candidate.",
